            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "3. Take two cardboard cutouts of a scalene triangle with sides 6 cm, 9 cm, and 12 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.131,
            0.474,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_23_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.525,
            0.13,
            0.829,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_23_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.233,
            0.848,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "？ What are the different ways they can be joined to get a quadrilateral?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.188,
            0.982,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_23_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.269,
            0.849,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "？ Are you able to identify the different quadrilaterals that are obtained by joining the triangles? Justify your answer whenever you identify a quadrilateral."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.349,
            0.475,
            0.374
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.6 Kite and Trapezium"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.386,
            0.209,
            0.407
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Kite"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.417,
            0.848,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "One of the ways the two triangles of sides 6 cm, 9 cm and 12 cm can be joined together is as follows —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.456,
            0.604,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_23_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.897,
            0.354,
            0.995,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_23_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.711,
            0.659,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "This quadrilateral looks like a kite. Observe that the adjacent sides are of the same length."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.753,
            0.659,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "**Kite:** A kite is a quadrilateral that can be labelled ABCD such that $\\mathbf { A } \\bar { \\mathbf { B } } = \\mathbf { B } \\mathbf { C } ,$ and $\\mathrm { C D } = \\mathrm { D A }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.825,
            0.557,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": "(i) bisects $\\angle  ABC$ and $\\angle  ADC ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.848,
            0.659,
            0.906
          ],
          "content": "(ii) bisects the diagonal AC, that is, $\\mathrm { A O } = \\mathrm { O C } ,$ and is perpendicular to it. **Hint:** Is $\\Delta  AOB  \\cong \\Delta  COB?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.678,
            0.554,
            1.0,
            0.941
          ],
          "content": "",
